	"gamemechanicinfo-siding-title": "VS Siding Mod: Guide",
	"gamemechanicinfo-siding-text": "<strong>VS Siding Mod: Guide</strong><br>A siding wall is a thin panel, a quarter of a block thick, built up in layers: a <strong>framing</strong> timber, an <strong>infill</strong> between the timbers, and a <strong>finish</strong> on each face. Framing plus infill is a complete wall, and it seals a room the way a solid block does. Finishes are appearance only.<br><br><strong>Building one</strong><br>Put a <i>saw</i> in your off-hand slot and hold the material in your main hand, then <strong>right-click</strong> the face of the block where the wall should stand. Every layer goes on the same way: right-click while the saw is in your off hand.<br><br><i>Planks</i> build the framing. Right-click the standing frame with an infill material, such as <i>sticks (wattle)</i>, <i>straw</i>, <i>clay</i>, <i>rubble stone</i> or <i>glass</i>, to fill it. Right-click a filled wall with a finish material to face that side. <i>Glass</i> is the exception: a glazed wall takes no finish, because a board over it would only hide the glass.<br><br><strong>Saw modes</strong><br>The saw carries a tool mode picker, and the mode decides what you build.<br>• <strong>Wall</strong>: a flat panel across the cell.<br>• <strong>Corner</strong>: an outside corner, two faces meeting. Right-clicking a bare frame in this mode upgrades it in place, so there is no need to break it first.<br>• <strong>Weatherboard</strong>: overlapping tapered boards. <i>Plank finishes only.</i><br>• <strong>Flat boards</strong>: plain flush boards. <i>Plank finishes only.</i><br>Stone, brick and daub finishes have no board profile, so these two modes refuse them.<br><br><strong>Rooms and cellars</strong><br>Heat retention and cellar cooling work as they would with a solid block. The infill decides whether it counts as a <i>cooling</i> wall: <i>rubble stone</i> and <i>clay</i> do, <i>sticks (wattle)</i> and <i>straw</i> do not. <i>Glass</i> infill seals the same way but lets daylight straight through, and neighbouring glazed cells merge into one pane.<br><br><strong>Taking one apart</strong><br>Breaking a wall peels one layer at a time, outermost first, on the face you hit: the finish, then the infill, then the framing. The material comes back each time.<br><br><strong>Quirks</strong><br>A wall fills only a quarter of its cell, and its open side sits empty. Right-click that side with a chest, pot, torch, sign or other piece of furniture and it takes the cell for itself; the wall stays put behind it and keeps sealing the room as before. Its layers can't be changed while something stands in front of it: break the furniture first and the wall comes back. A trunk laid along a straight run of wall takes both walls' cells the same way."
}
